  "authoringNote": "This is a missing-date vector, not an outside-interval conflict or a C01 boundary proof. The only order is final and usable but states orderDate null and orderTime null. R03 holds R03.element_missing for element.order_date on that order (missing, authorization coordinator) and cites its other eight element facts, including the signature signer name and signed date, which remain signature metadata only and never stand in for the order date. R04 holds R04.date_not_stated with qualifier order.date naming the order (missing, authorization coordinator); the pair is not compared by date, and the identifiers match. R04 cites the encounter status, date and practitioner identifier and the order status and practitioner identifier (five). The receipt is current, usable, references the exact order version and states a receipt time, so it is linked and no order_receipt_missing holds; with neither an order date nor a timestamp there is no C06 span, and the pack declares such a receipt not sequenced, so R05 holds R05.receipt_unsequenced naming the order and the receipt, manual_review for the administrative reviewer. R05 cites only the receipt reference and time (three); no order date or timestamp fact exists. No rule reads the metadata authoredAt as the order date. No parent is named (C07: no restricted, unreadable or failed-provenance condition). R01, R02, R07, R08 and R09 pass, R06 is qualified clinical review with five clinical citations and no administrative effect, and R10 is not applicable. The highest effect is R05 needs_human_review; the R03 and R04 missing conditions keep their coordinator routing beneath it.",
